{
  "module": "The transformer block",
  "lesson": {
    "slug": "residual-stream",
    "title": "The residual connection and the residual stream",
    "summary": "Why each layer adds its output back onto its input, y = x + F(x): an identity path for gradients, layers that learn corrections, and the view of one stream every layer reads from and adds to.",
    "covers": ["residual-connection"],
    "prerequisites": [
      {
        "lesson": "layers-hidden-states",
        "reason": "The residual connection adds a layer's output to its input, the hidden states."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "gradient-descent",
        "reason": "Its purpose is about how gradients travel back through the layers."
      }
    ]
  },
  "screens": [
    {
      "ref": "deep-problem",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Training sends gradients "
            },
            {
              "text": "backward",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " through every layer, multiplying by each layer's effect on its input. Through many layers that is a long product."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "A toy case: if each of 3 layers changes its output by only 0.1 for each unit of input, a gradient is multiplied by 0.1 × 0.1 × 0.1 = 0.001 on its way back. It has almost vanished."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Real models have dozens of layers, so this is a real danger for a deep stack."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 55, 11)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "add-it-back",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The remedy is to "
            },
            {
              "text": "add each layer's input back onto its output",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ". If a layer computes F(x) from its input x, the layer's result is not F(x) but:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "latex": "y = x + F(x)",
          "spoken": "y equals x plus F of x"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "This is a "
            },
            {
              "text": "residual connection",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": "."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 55)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "identity-path",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Now look at the gradient. Because of the added x, the effect of the input on the result is "
            },
            {
              "text": "1 + F′(x)",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ", where the 1 comes from x itself."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "That 1 is an "
            },
            {
              "text": "identity path",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ": it passes gradients straight through whatever F does."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "In the toy case, each layer now multiplies the gradient by 1 + 0.1 = 1.1, and over 3 layers that is 1.1³ = 1.331, instead of 0.001. The gradient survives the whole depth."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 55)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "learn-a-correction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "It changes what a layer has to learn. With y = x + F(x), the layer learns a "
            },
            {
              "text": "correction",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " to add to the vectors it receives, and not a whole new representation."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "A toy case: x = (1.0, 2.0) and F(x) = (0.5, -1.0) give y = (1.5, 1.0). The input is kept, and adjusted a little."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 55)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "the-stream",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Follow this through a whole stack and there is a neat picture: a "
            },
            {
              "text": "single n × d_model tensor",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " flows through the model, and every layer "
            },
            {
              "text": "reads from it and adds its output back to it",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "This is the "
            },
            {
              "text": "residual stream",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " view. The stream is never replaced, only added to, layer after layer."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "What F is inside a real layer, an attention step and a feed-forward step, is the subject of the next lessons. The residual connection works the same whatever F is."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 55)"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "questions": [
